<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="u6-constraints-svd" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Constrained optimization and Lagrange multipliers</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="def-8-1-optimization"/> that an optimization problem has an objective function and a feasible region. In a constrained optimization problem, one or more equations or inequalities restrict the feasible region.
    </p>
    <p>
      <xref ref="sec-maxima-minima-problems" text="custom">The section “Optimization problems and critical points”</xref> found interior critical points by solving
      <me>
        \nabla f(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{0}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      On a closed bounded region, absolute extrema may also occur on the boundary. Lagrange multipliers give equations for critical points on boundaries and feasible sets described by one or more equality constraints.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same method connects constrained optimization to linear algebra. On the unit sphere, the extrema of a symmetric quadratic form occur in eigenvector directions. This leads to <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis" text="custom">the section “Principal component analysis”</xref> for centered data and <xref ref="sec-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">the section “Singular value decomposition”</xref> for general matrix maps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Big questions.</em> How do we find absolute extrema when the allowable inputs are restricted? Why do eigenvectors identify extremal directions of quadratic forms, data sets, and matrix maps?
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Learning outcomes.</em> By the end of this unit, students should be able to:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO1.</em> Find absolute extrema on closed bounded regions by comparing interior and boundary candidates.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO2.</em> Formulate and solve Lagrange-multiplier problems with one equality constraint, and explain the parallel-gradient and Lagrangian formulations.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO3.</em> Formulate and solve Lagrange-multiplier problems with several equality constraints, and interpret the objective gradient as a linear combination of constraint gradients.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO4.</em> Use Lagrange multipliers and eigenvalues to find extrema of symmetric quadratic forms on the unit sphere, and apply this result to maximum stretch and singular values.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO5.</em> Center a small data set, form its covariance matrix, compute and interpret first and second principal directions, scores, reconstructions, and captured variation, and distinguish PCA from linear regression.</p></li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>U6-LO6.</em> Construct and interpret a small singular value decomposition
          <me>
            A=U\Sigma V^T,
          </me>
          including its orthonormal input and output directions and singular-value stretch factors.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO7.</em> Use an SVD to identify rank and orthonormal bases for the four fundamental subspaces, and connect the SVD of centered data with PCA directions, scores, and reconstructions.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Toolbox skills.</em> Compare interior and boundary candidates; restrict a function to simple boundary pieces; form and solve one- and several-constraint Lagrange systems; write and differentiate Lagrangians; evaluate and diagonalize symmetric quadratic forms; center data and form covariance matrices; compute principal scores, projections, and reconstruction residuals; form <m>A^TA</m>; compute singular values and singular vectors; assemble and read small SVDs; and identify rank and bases for the four fundamental subspaces.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="sec-u6-absolute-extrema.ptx" />

  <xi:include href="sec-u6-lagrange-multipliers.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-maximum-stretch-svd">
    <title>Quadratic forms and maximum stretch</title>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="def-u5-quadratic-form"/> that an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>B</m> determines the scalar-valued function
      <me>
        q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.
      </me>
      In this section, <m>B</m> will be symmetric. By <xref ref="thm-principal-axes"/>, <m>B</m> has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. Lagrange multipliers give these eigenvectors an optimization interpretation.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors">
      <title>Extrema of a symmetric quadratic form on the unit sphere</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>B</m> be a symmetric <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and let
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
          If <m>\lambda_{\min}</m> and <m>\lambda_{\max}</m> are the smallest and largest eigenvalues of <m>B</m>, then
          <me>
            \min_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}q_B(\mathbf{x})
            =
            \lambda_{\min}
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}q_B(\mathbf{x})
            =
            \lambda_{\max}.
          </me>
          These values are achieved at unit eigenvectors for <m>\lambda_{\min}</m> and <m>\lambda_{\max}</m>. More generally, every constrained critical point is a unit eigenvector of <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Use the constraint
          <me>
            g(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^T\mathbf{x}=1.
          </me>
          Since <m>B</m> is symmetric,
          <me>
            \nabla q_B(\mathbf{x})=2B\mathbf{x},
            \qquad
            \nabla g(\mathbf{x})=2\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
          On the unit sphere, <m>\nabla g(\mathbf{x})\neq\mathbf{0}</m>, so <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier"/> applies. Its equations are
          <me>
            2B\mathbf{x}=2\mu\mathbf{x},
          </me>
          or
          <me>
            B\mathbf{x}=\mu\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
          Thus the constrained critical points are unit eigenvectors of <m>B</m>. In this calculation, the Lagrange multiplier <m>\mu</m> is the corresponding eigenvalue.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>B\mathbf{x}=\lambda\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\|\mathbf{x}\|=1</m>, then
          <md>
            <mrow>q_B(\mathbf{x})</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^T(\lambda\mathbf{x})</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lambda\|\mathbf{x}\|^2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lambda.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The unit sphere is closed and bounded, so absolute extrema exist by <xref ref="thm-u6-extreme-value"/>. Comparing the eigenvalues gives the smallest and largest values.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-principal-coordinate-view">
      <title>Principal-coordinate view</title>
      <p>
        The Lagrange calculation identifies the critical directions. Orthogonal diagonalization explains all possible values of the quadratic form.
      </p>
      <p>
        By <xref ref="thm-principal-axes"/>, write
        <me>
          B=QDQ^T,
          \qquad
          D=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \lambda_1 \amp \amp 0\\
          \amp \ddots \amp\\
          0 \amp \amp \lambda_n
          \end{bmatrix},
        </me>
        where the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal eigenvectors of <m>B</m>. Set
        <me>
          \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x}.
        </me>
        Since <m>Q</m> is orthogonal, <xref ref="lem-orthogonal-matrix-properties"/> gives
        <me>
          \|\mathbf{c}\|=\|\mathbf{x}\|.
        </me>
        Also,
        <md>
          <mrow>q_B(\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{c}^TD\mathbf{c}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\lambda_1c_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_nc_n^2.</mrow>
        </md>
        On the unit sphere,
        <me>
          c_1^2+\cdots+c_n^2=1.
        </me>
        Thus <m>q_B(\mathbf{x})</m> is a weighted average of the eigenvalues, with nonnegative weights <m>c_i^2</m>. It must lie between the smallest and largest eigenvalues.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-non-diagonal-quadratic-form-unit-circle">
      <title>A non-diagonal quadratic form on the unit circle</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to the symmetric matrix from <xref ref="ex-symmetric-matrix-2x2"/>:
          <me>
            B=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x\\
            y
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Write <m>q_B(x,y)</m> in scalar form.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Set up the Lagrange equations for finding the extrema of <m>q_B</m> subject to
              <me>
                x^2+y^2=1.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Find all constrained critical points and their <m>q_B</m>-values.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find an orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m> and diagonal matrix <m>D</m> such that
              <me>
                B=QDQ^T,
              </me>
              with the larger eigenvalue first.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If
              <me>
                \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x},
              </me>
              rewrite <m>q_B</m> in principal coordinates and use this form to identify its absolute maximum and minimum.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO4, U5-LO4.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="sol-u6-non-diagonal-quadratic-form-unit-circle">
        <p>
          We have
          <md>
            <mrow>q_B(x,y)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x \amp y
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x\\
            y
            \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=2x^2-2xy+2y^2.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Its gradient is
          <me>
            \nabla q_B(x,y)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4x-2y\\
            -2x+4y
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            g(x,y)=x^2+y^2,
          </me>
          we have
          <me>
            \nabla g(x,y)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2x\\
            2y
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The Lagrange equations are therefore
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4x-2y\\
            -2x+4y
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \mu
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2x\\
            2y
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            x^2+y^2=1.
          </me>
          Dividing the gradient equation by <m>2</m> gives
          <me>
            B\mathbf{x}=\mu\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The characteristic polynomial is
          <md>
            <mrow>\det(B-\mu I)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=(2-\mu)^2-1</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=(\mu-1)(\mu-3).</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\mu=1</m>, the eigenvectors satisfy <m>x=y</m>. The corresponding unit vectors are
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          At either point,
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\mu=3</m>, the eigenvectors satisfy <m>x=-y</m>. The corresponding unit vectors are
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          At either point,
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=3.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Choose
          <me>
            Q=
            \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1\\
            -1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            D=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal eigenvectors of <m>B</m>, so
          <me>
            B=QDQ^T.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          If
          <me>
            \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x},
          </me>
          then
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=3c_1^2+c_2^2.
          </me>
          On the unit circle,
          <me>
            c_1^2+c_2^2=1.
          </me>
          Therefore the absolute maximum is <m>3</m>, attained when <m>c_1^2=1</m>, and the absolute minimum is <m>1</m>, attained when <m>c_2^2=1</m>. These are the same values and directions found from the Lagrange equations.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Maximum stretch of a matrix</title>
      <p>
        Now let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix. The matrix <m>A</m> need not be square or symmetric. One way to study the matrix map
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}\longmapsto A\mathbf{x}
        </me>
        is to ask which unit inputs produce the largest output lengths.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because lengths are nonnegative, maximizing <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|</m> is equivalent to maximizing its square. We have
        <md>
          <mrow>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=(A\mathbf{x})^T(A\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^TA^TA\mathbf{x}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Thus maximum stretch is a quadratic-form problem for the symmetric matrix <m>A^TA</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> are nonnegative. Indeed, if
        <me>
          A^TA\mathbf{v}=\lambda\mathbf{v}
        </me>
        for a nonzero vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, then
        <md>
          <mrow>\lambda\|\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{v}^TA^TA\mathbf{v}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\|A\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp\geq 0.</mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore <m>\lambda\geq 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Order the eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> from largest to smallest and take their nonnegative square roots:
        <me>
          \sigma_i=\sqrt{\lambda_i}.
        </me>
        These numbers are the singular values of <m>A</m>. A unit eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}_i</m> of <m>A^TA</m> is a right singular vector of <m>A</m>. Since
        <md>
          <mrow>\|A\mathbf{v}_i\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{v}_i^TA^TA\mathbf{v}_i</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\lambda_i,</mrow>
        </md>
        we have
        <me>
          \|A\mathbf{v}_i\|=\sigma_i.
        </me>
        Thus the right singular vectors are special input directions, and the singular values are their stretch factors. In particular,
        <me>
          \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|
          =
          \sigma_1.
        </me>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-small-svd-stretch">
      <title>A small stretch computation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{e}_1=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{e}_2=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>A\mathbf{e}_2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\|A\mathbf{e}_1\|</m> and <m>\|A\mathbf{e}_2\|</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>What are the singular values of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which unit direction is stretched most?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            A\mathbf{e}_1=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            A\mathbf{e}_2=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \|A\mathbf{e}_1\|=3,
            \qquad
            \|A\mathbf{e}_2\|=1.
          </me>
          Also
          <me>
            A^TA=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}^T
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            9 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> are <m>9</m> and <m>1</m>. Therefore the singular values of <m>A</m> are
          <me>
            \sigma_1=3,
            \qquad
            \sigma_2=1.
          </me>
          The direction <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> is stretched most. This is the simplest version of the question
          <me>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-reading-maximum-stretch-code">
      <title>Reading the same stretch in code</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following code repeats the previous activity using <m>A^TA</m>.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

A = np.array([[3., 0.],
              [0., 1.]])

B = A.T @ A
eigvals, eigvecs = np.linalg.eigh(B)

singular_values = np.sqrt(eigvals[::-1])
dominant_direction = eigvecs[:, -1]

B, eigvals, singular_values, dominant_direction
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <pre><![CDATA[(array([[9., 0.],
        [0., 1.]]),
 array([1., 9.]),
 array([3., 1.]),
 array([1., 0.]))]]></pre>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What mathematical matrix is stored in <c>B</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why are the entries of <c>eigvals</c> squared stretch factors?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does the code take a square root?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does <c>eigvecs[:, -1]</c> give the most-stretched input direction?</p></li>
          <li><p>What value of <m>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|</m> does the code predict?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrix <c>B</c> is <m>A^TA</m>. The entries of <c>eigvals</c> are eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>, so they are values of <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</m> for special unit input directions. The code takes a square root because singular values measure <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|</m>, not <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</m>. The command <c>np.linalg.eigh</c> lists eigenvalues in increasing order, so the largest eigenvalue is last, and <c>eigvecs[:, -1]</c> is its eigenvector. The largest singular value is <m>3</m>, so
          <me>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|=3.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      The same quadratic-form problem appears when a matrix stores centered data. <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis"/> uses it to choose directions that capture variation in a data set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis">
    <title>Principal component analysis</title>

    <p>
      A data matrix can have many feature columns. Principal component analysis, or PCA, looks for orthogonal directions that capture as much centered variation as possible. The first direction is found with one constraint: it must have unit length. Each later direction has additional orthogonality constraints.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-u6-centered-data-covariance">
      <title>Centered data and covariance matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{x}_n\in\R^d
          </me>
          be data vectors. Their mean is
          <me>
            \bar{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf{x}_i.
          </me>
          The centered data vectors are
          <me>
            \mathbf{z}_i
            =
            \mathbf{x}_i-\bar{\mathbf{x}}.
          </me>
          Let <m>Z</m> be the <m>n\times d</m> matrix whose <m>i</m>-th row is <m>\mathbf{z}_i^T</m>. The covariance matrix is
          <md>
            <mrow>C</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac1n Z^TZ</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{z}_i\mathbf{z}_i^T.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Some conventions divide by <m>n-1</m> instead of <m>n</m>. This multiplies every covariance eigenvalue by the same positive constant and does not change the eigenvectors.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-scores-captured-variation">
      <title>Scores and captured variation</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}\in\R^d</m> be a unit vector. The score of the <m>i</m>-th centered data point in direction <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
        <me>
          t_i
          =
          \mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{z}_i.
        </me>
        The score vector is
        <me>
          \mathbf{t}=Z\mathbf{v}.
        </me>
        Its average squared score is
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n t_i^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\frac1n\|Z\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Thus captured variation is a quadratic form.
      </p>
      <p>
        The matrix <m>C</m> is symmetric. Also,
        <md>
          <mrow>\mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\frac1n\|Z\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp\geq 0</mrow>
        </md>
        for every <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, so the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> are nonnegative.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <definition xml:id="def-u6-first-principal-direction">
      <title>First principal direction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A first principal direction is a unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> that maximizes
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
          </me>
          subject to
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{v}\|=1.
          </me>
          The entries of
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_1=Z\mathbf{v}_1
          </me>
          are the first principal-component scores.
        </p>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors"/>, <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> may be chosen as a unit eigenvector of <m>C</m> corresponding to its largest eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1</m>. The maximum captured variation is <m>\lambda_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-reconstruction-view">
      <title>Reconstruction view</title>
      <p>
        For a unit direction <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, the orthogonal projection of <m>\mathbf{z}_i</m> onto
        <me>
          \spans\{\mathbf{v}\}
        </me>
        is
        <me>
          \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i
          =
          t_i\mathbf{v}.
        </me>
        The corresponding reconstructed data point is
        <me>
          \widehat{\mathbf{x}}_i
          =
          \bar{\mathbf{x}}+\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i.
        </me>
        Define the reconstruction residual by
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}_i
          =
          \mathbf{z}_i-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i.
        </me>
        By <xref ref="def-orthogonal-projection"/> and <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>,
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}_i\perp\mathbf{v}.
        </me>
        Therefore
        <me>
          \|\mathbf{z}_i\|^2
          =
          t_i^2+\|\mathbf{r}_i\|^2.
        </me>
        Summing over the data gives
        <me>
          \sum_{i=1}^n\|\mathbf{z}_i\|^2
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^n t_i^2
          +
          \sum_{i=1}^n\|\mathbf{r}_i\|^2.
        </me>
        The quantity on the left does not depend on <m>\mathbf{v}</m>. Thus maximizing the total squared scores is equivalent to minimizing the total squared reconstruction residuals.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-pca-small-data-set">
      <title>Principal directions of a small data set</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The rows of
          <me>
            X=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4 \amp 3\\
            3 \amp 4\\
            0 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          are four data points in <m>\R^2</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute the mean <m>\bar{\mathbf{x}}</m> and the centered data matrix <m>Z</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                C=\frac14Z^TZ.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. Choose <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> for the larger eigenvalue and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> for the smaller eigenvalue.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the first principal-component score vector
              <me>
                \mathbf{t}_1=Z\mathbf{v}_1.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For the first centered point <m>\mathbf{z}_1</m>, compute
              <me>
                \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1
                =
                (\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1)\mathbf{v}_1
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}_1
                =
                \mathbf{z}_1-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1.
              </me>
              Check that
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_1=0
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \|\mathbf{z}_1\|^2
                =
                (\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1)^2+\|\mathbf{r}_1\|^2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Form
              <me>
                V=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \mathbf{v}_2
                \end{bmatrix}
              </me>
              and compute <m>V^TCV</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Describe the affine line
              <me>
                \bar{\mathbf{x}}+\spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO5, U5-LO4, U4-LO3.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="sol-u6-pca-small-data-set">
        <p>
          The mean is
          <me>
            \bar{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            Z=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp 2\\
            -2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We calculate
          <md>
            <mrow>C</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac14Z^TZ</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac14
            \begin{bmatrix}
            10 \amp 8\\
            8 \amp 10
            \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5/2 \amp 2\\
            2 \amp 5/2
            \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The eigenvalues are
          <me>
            \lambda_1=\frac92,
            \qquad
            \lambda_2=\frac12.
          </me>
          Corresponding unit eigenvectors are
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_1
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_2
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first score vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_1
            =
            Z\mathbf{v}_1
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            3\\
            -3\\
            -3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            \mathbf{z}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          the score is
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1
            =
            \frac3{\sqrt2}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1
            =
            \frac3{\sqrt2}\mathbf{v}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2\\
            3/2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1/2\\
            -1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_1
            =
            0.
          </me>
          Also,
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{z}_1\|^2=5,
            \qquad
            (\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1)^2=\frac92,
            \qquad
            \|\mathbf{r}_1\|^2=\frac12,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            5=\frac92+\frac12.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          With
          <me>
            V
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp -1
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          we obtain
          <me>
            V^TCV
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            9/2 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first direction captures the fraction
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{\lambda_1}{\lambda_1+\lambda_2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac{9/2}{9/2+1/2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac9{10}</mrow>
          </md>
          of the total centered variation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally,
          <me>
            \bar{\mathbf{x}}
            +
            \spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}
            =
            \left\{
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}
            +
            s
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}
            :
            s\in\R
            \right\}.
          </me>
          This is the line <m>y=x</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <definition xml:id="def-u6-second-principal-direction">
      <title>Second principal direction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is a first principal direction. A second principal direction is a unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> that maximizes
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
          </me>
          subject to the two constraints
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}=1
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}_1=0.
          </me>
          In <m>\R^2</m>, the second constraint leaves only one axis, up to sign. In higher dimensions, it restricts the optimization to the unit vectors orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u6-second-principal-direction-eigenvector">
      <title>The second principal direction is another eigenvector</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> be ordered so that
          <me>
            \lambda_1\geq\lambda_2\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_d.
          </me>
          If <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is a unit eigenvector for <m>\lambda_1</m>, then a second principal direction may be chosen as a unit eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> for <m>\lambda_2</m> that is orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          On the feasible set, the gradients of the two constraints are
          <me>
            2\mathbf{v}
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            \mathbf{v}_1.
          </me>
          They are nonzero and orthogonal, so they are linearly independent by <xref ref="thm-6-1-orthogonality-independent"/>. Thus <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier-several-constraints"/> applies.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define
          <me>
            L(\mathbf{v},\mu,\nu)
            =
            \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
            -
            \mu(\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}-1)
            -
            \nu\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}_1.
          </me>
          The stationarity equation is
          <me>
            2C\mathbf{v}
            =
            2\mu\mathbf{v}+\nu\mathbf{v}_1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Take the dot product with <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. Since <m>C</m> is symmetric,
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v}_1^TC\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=(C\mathbf{v}_1)^T\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lambda_1\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=0.</mrow>
          </md>
          The first constraint term also has zero dot product with <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. Since
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{v}_1\|=1,
          </me>
          it follows that <m>\nu=0</m>. Hence
          <me>
            C\mathbf{v}=\mu\mathbf{v}.
          </me>
          Thus a constrained critical direction is an eigenvector of <m>C</m>. Among the eigenvectors orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>, the largest remaining value of the quadratic form is <m>\lambda_2</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <p>
      More generally, the <m>j</m>-th principal direction maximizes
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
      </me>
      subject to unit length and orthogonality to
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{j-1}.
      </me>
      The same argument gives a unit eigenvector for <m>\lambda_j</m>. If an eigenvalue is repeated, the individual principal directions inside its eigenspace are not unique, but their span is fixed.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-principal-coordinates">
      <title>Principal coordinates</title>
      <p>
        Collect an orthonormal set of principal directions as the columns of
        <me>
          V=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{v}_d
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        By <xref ref="thm-principal-axes"/>,
        <me>
          V^TCV
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \lambda_1 \amp \amp 0\\
          \amp \ddots \amp\\
          0 \amp \amp \lambda_d
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        The principal-coordinate matrix is
        <me>
          T=ZV.
        </me>
        Its <m>j</m>-th column contains the scores in direction <m>\mathbf{v}_j</m>, and
        <me>
          \frac1nT^TT
          =
          V^TCV.
        </me>
        Thus <m>\lambda_j</m> is the average squared score in the <m>j</m>-th principal direction, while the off-diagonal entries are zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        The total centered variation is
        <me>
          \lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d.
        </me>
        When the centered data are not all zero, the fraction captured by the first <m>k</m> principal directions is
        <me>
          \frac{\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_k}
          {\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d}.
        </me>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-linear-regression">
      <title>PCA and linear regression</title>
      <p>
        PCA and linear regression both use projection and sums of squared residuals, but they solve different problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        PCA treats the feature coordinates symmetrically. It chooses an affine subspace through the data mean, and its reconstruction residuals are perpendicular to that subspace.
      </p>
      <p>
        Linear regression designates one coordinate as the response and minimizes vertical prediction residuals in the data plot. Algebraically, it projects the response vector onto the column space of a design matrix, as in <xref ref="sec-u4-least-squares-as-projection"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Thus PCA and linear regression can choose different best-fit lines for the same data.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      For PCA, the principal directions come from
      <me>
        C=\frac1nZ^TZ.
      </me>
      <xref ref="sec-singular-value-decomposition"/> packages the corresponding input directions, output directions, and stretch factors directly from <m>Z</m>, and extends the same picture to an arbitrary rectangular matrix.
    </p>
  </section>

  <xi:include href="sec-u6-singular-value-decomposition.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-u6-svd-information-channels">
    <title>PCA from the SVD</title>

    <p>
      <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis"/> found principal directions by diagonalizing the covariance matrix
      <me>
        C=\frac1nZ^TZ.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The SVD of the centered data matrix <m>Z</m> contains the same directions and the same captured-variation values. It also connects principal-component scores with left singular vectors.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u6-pca-from-centered-data-svd">
      <title>PCA from a centered-data SVD</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            Z\in\R^{n\times d}
          </me>
          be a centered data matrix, and let
          <me>
            Z=U\Sigma V^T
          </me>
          be <xref ref="def-u6-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">an SVD</xref>. Write
          <me>
            V=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{v}_d
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and include zero singular values when needed. Then
          <me>
            C=\frac1nZ^TZ
            =
            V\left(\frac1n\Sigma^T\Sigma\right)V^T.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>Therefore:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>the right singular vectors <m>\mathbf{v}_j</m> are principal directions;</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the covariance eigenvalues are
              <me>
                \lambda_j=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{n};
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the principal-coordinate matrix is
              <me>
                T=ZV=U\Sigma.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In particular, the <m>j</m>-th score vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_j=\sigma_j\mathbf{u}_j
          </me>
          when <m>\sigma_j&gt;0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since <m>U</m> is orthogonal,
          <me>
            U^TU=I.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <md>
            <mrow>Z^TZ</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            (U\Sigma V^T)^T(U\Sigma V^T)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            V\Sigma^TU^TU\Sigma V^T</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            V\Sigma^T\Sigma V^T.</mrow>
          </md>
          Dividing by <m>n</m> gives the displayed orthogonal diagonalization of <m>C</m>. The columns of <m>V</m> are therefore covariance eigenvectors, with eigenvalues <m>\sigma_j^2/n</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also,
          <md>
            <mrow>ZV</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            U\Sigma V^TV</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            U\Sigma.</mrow>
          </md>
          Thus the columns of <m>U\Sigma</m> are the principal-component score vectors.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-keeping-first-k-directions">
      <title>Keeping the first <m>k</m> principal directions</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>p=\min(n,d)</m> and <m>1\leq k\leq p</m>. Set
        <me>
          V_k=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{v}_k
          \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          T_k=ZV_k,
        </me>
        where <m>V_k\in\R^{d\times k}</m> and <m>T_k\in\R^{n\times k}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The centered-data reconstruction using the first <m>k</m> principal directions is
        <me>
          \widehat Z_k
          =
          T_kV_k^T
          =
          ZV_kV_k^T.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the columns of <m>V_k</m> are orthonormal, each row of <m>\widehat Z_k</m> is the orthogonal projection of the corresponding centered data vector onto
        <me>
          \spans
          \{\mathbf{v}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{v}_k\};
        </me>
        see <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>U_k\in\R^{n\times k}</m> contains the first <m>k</m> left singular vectors and
        <me>
          \Sigma_k
          =
          \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_k)
          \in\R^{k\times k},
        </me>
        then
        <me>
          \widehat Z_k
          =
          U_k\Sigma_kV_k^T.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The reconstruction has rank at most <m>k</m>. Each <m>d</m>-dimensional centered data point is represented by its <m>k</m> scores and reconstructed from those scores.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fraction of total centered variation captured is
        <me>
          \frac{\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_k}
          {\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d}
          =
          \frac{\sigma_1^2+\cdots+\sigma_k^2}
          {\sigma_1^2+\cdots+\sigma_p^2},
        </me>
        provided the centered data are not all zero.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-pca-small-data-set-through-svd">
      <title>The small PCA data set through the SVD</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>. There,
          <me>
            Z=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp 2\\
            -2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            V=
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp -1
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and the covariance eigenvalues are
          <me>
            \lambda_1=\frac92,
            \qquad
            \lambda_2=\frac12.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use
              <me>
                \lambda_j=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{4}
              </me>
              to find the singular values of <m>Z</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the principal-coordinate matrix
              <me>
                T=ZV.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>\mathbf{t}_1,\mathbf{t}_2</m> be the columns of <m>T</m>. Compute
              <me>
                \mathbf{u}_1=\frac{\mathbf{t}_1}{\sigma_1},
                \qquad
                \mathbf{u}_2=\frac{\mathbf{t}_2}{\sigma_2},
              </me>
              and verify that <m>\mathbf{u}_1,\mathbf{u}_2</m> are orthonormal.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Set
              <me>
                U_2=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{u}_1 \amp \mathbf{u}_2
                \end{bmatrix},
                \qquad
                \Sigma_2=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \sigma_1 \amp 0\\
                0 \amp \sigma_2
                \end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
              Explain why
              <me>
                Z=U_2\Sigma_2V^T.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the reconstruction using only the first principal direction:
              <me>
                \widehat Z_1
                =
                \mathbf{t}_1\mathbf{v}_1^T.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Interpret the rows of <m>\widehat Z_1</m>. How does its first row compare with the projection computed in the earlier PCA activity?</p></li>
          <li><p>Use the singular values to compute the fraction of total centered variation captured by the first principal direction.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO5, U6-LO7, U4-LO3.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="sol-u6-pca-small-data-set-through-svd">
        <p>
          Since <m>n=4</m>,
          <me>
            \sigma_1
            =
            \sqrt{4\lambda_1}
            =
            \sqrt{18}
            =
            3\sqrt2,
            \qquad
            \sigma_2
            =
            \sqrt{4\lambda_2}
            =
            \sqrt2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We compute
          <me>
            T=ZV
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 1\\
            3 \amp -1\\
            -3 \amp -1\\
            -3 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_1
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            3\\
            -3\\
            -3
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{t}_2
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            -1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Dividing by the singular values gives
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_1
            =
            \frac12
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1\\
            -1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{u}_2
            =
            \frac12
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            -1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Both vectors have norm <m>1</m>, and
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_1\cdot\mathbf{u}_2=0.
          </me>
          Therefore they are orthonormal.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            T=U_2\Sigma_2.
          </me>
          Since
          <me>
            T=ZV
          </me>
          and <m>V</m> is orthogonal,
          <me>
            Z
            =
            TV^T
            =
            U_2\Sigma_2V^T.
          </me>
          This factorization uses the two left singular vectors needed to reconstruct <m>Z</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keeping only the first principal direction gives
          <me>
            \widehat Z_1
            =
            \mathbf{t}_1\mathbf{v}_1^T
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2 \amp 3/2\\
            3/2 \amp 3/2\\
            -3/2 \amp -3/2\\
            -3/2 \amp -3/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Each row is the projection of the corresponding centered data vector onto
          <me>
            \spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.
          </me>
          In particular, the first row is
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2 \amp 3/2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          which is the transpose of the projected centered vector
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2\\
            3/2
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          found in the earlier PCA activity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally,
          <me>
            \frac{\sigma_1^2}
            {\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2}
            =
            \frac{18}{18+2}
            =
            \frac9{10}.
          </me>
          Thus the first principal direction captures <m>90\%</m> of the total centered variation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u6-apps-computation">
    <title>Coding recap</title>
    <subsection xml:id="lab-u6-callout">
      <title>Linked notebook</title>
      <p>
        Run <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u6">Lab U6: Constraints, PCA, and SVD</url>. The lab checks one- and several-constraint Lagrange conditions, connects quadratic forms with maximum stretch, computes PCA from covariance and from the SVD, compares PCA with linear regression, and reads rank and the four fundamental subspaces from an SVD.
      </p>
    <p>
        For a quick reference on arrays and shapes, matrix products, numerical checks, eigenvalue and SVD commands, and plotting, see the programming appendix sections <xref ref="appcomp-arrays-shapes" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-elementwise-vs-linear-algebra" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-numerical-checks" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-numpy-linalg" text="global"/>, and <xref ref="appcomp-plotting" text="global"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-u6-review-activities">
      <title>Review activities</title>
      <p>
        These activities interpret Python code and output for PCA and SVD: centering data, covariance matrices, principal directions, reconstruction, reduced-SVD shapes, and singular-vector identities. Reopen <xref ref="def-u6-first-principal-direction" text="custom">the definition “First principal direction”</xref> or <xref ref="def-u6-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">the definition “Singular value decomposition”</xref> as needed.
      </p>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-reading-pca-computation">
        <title>Reading a PCA computation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code repeats part of <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

np.set_printoptions(precision=3, suppress=True)

X = np.array([
    [4.0, 3.0],
    [3.0, 4.0],
    [0.0, 1.0],
    [1.0, 0.0],
])

xbar = X.mean(axis=0)
Z = X - xbar
C = Z.T @ Z / len(X)

eigvals, V = np.linalg.eigh(C)
order = np.argsort(eigvals)[::-1]
eigvals = eigvals[order]
V = V[:, order]

v1 = V[:, 0]
scores1 = Z @ v1
Zhat1 = np.outer(scores1, v1)
R1 = Z - Zhat1

(
    xbar,
    C,
    eigvals,
    V,
    Zhat1,
    np.allclose(R1 @ v1, np.zeros(len(X))),
    V.T @ C @ V,
)
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre><![CDATA[(array([2., 2.]),
 array([[2.5, 2. ],
        [2. , 2.5]]),
 array([4.5, 0.5]),
 array([[ 0.707, -0.707],
        [ 0.707,  0.707]]),
 array([[ 1.5,  1.5],
        [ 1.5,  1.5],
        [-1.5, -1.5],
        [-1.5, -1.5]]),
 True,
 array([[4.5, 0. ],
        [0. , 0.5]]))]]></pre>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Give the shapes of <c>X</c>, <c>xbar</c>, <c>Z</c>, <c>C</c>, <c>V</c>, <c>scores1</c>, and <c>Zhat1</c>.</p></li>
            <li><p>What mathematical objects are stored in <c>xbar</c> and <c>C</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does the code reverse the order returned by <c>np.linalg.eigh</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What do the columns of <c>V</c> represent?</p></li>
            <li><p>Interpret <c>scores1</c>, <c>Zhat1</c>, and <c>R1</c>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>What geometric condition is checked by</p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.allclose(R1 @ v1, np.zeros(len(X)))
]]></code></program>
              <p>?</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Another valid numerical output could replace a column of <c>V</c> by its negative. What would happen to the corresponding scores and reconstructions?</p></li>
            <li><p>Interpret the final diagonal matrix. What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO5.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution xml:id="sol-u6-recap-reading-pca-computation">
          <p>
            The shapes are
            <me>
              X\in\R^{4\times2},
              \qquad
              \bar{\mathbf{x}}\in\R^2,
              \qquad
              Z\in\R^{4\times2},
            </me>
            <me>
              C\in\R^{2\times2},
              \qquad
              V\in\R^{2\times2},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{t}_1\in\R^4,
              \qquad
              \widehat Z_1\in\R^{4\times2}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            The array <c>xbar</c> stores the sample mean
            <me>
              \bar{\mathbf{x}}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              2\\
              2
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            while <c>C</c> stores the covariance matrix
            <me>
              C=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              5/2 \amp 2\\
              2 \amp 5/2
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            The command <c>np.linalg.eigh</c> returns the eigenvalues in increasing order. The reversal places the largest eigenvalue first. The first column of <m>V</m> is therefore a first principal direction, and the second column is a second principal direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            The vector <c>scores1</c> stores
            <me>
              \mathbf{t}_1=Z\mathbf{v}_1.
            </me>
            The rows of <c>Zhat1</c> are the projections of the centered data vectors onto
            <me>
              \spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.
            </me>
            The matrix <c>R1</c> stores the corresponding reconstruction residuals.
          </p>
          <p>
            The product <c>R1 @ v1</c> computes the dot product of each residual row with <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. The value <c>True</c> checks that every reconstruction residual is numerically perpendicular to the first principal direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            Replacing <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> by <m>-\mathbf{v}_1</m> reverses the signs of its scores. However,
            <me>
              (-t_i)(-\mathbf{v}_1)=t_i\mathbf{v}_1,
            </me>
            so the projected points and reconstruction residuals do not change. The principal axis is the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            The final output says that covariance in the principal-coordinate system is
            <me>
              V^TCV
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              4.5 \amp 0\\
              0 \amp 0.5
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            The diagonal entries are the captured variations in the two principal directions. The first direction captures
            <me>
              \frac{4.5}{4.5+0.5}
              =
              \frac9{10}
            </me>
            of the total centered variation.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-svd-shapes">
        <title>Reading SVD shapes</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code computes a reduced SVD.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

A = np.array([[ 1., -1.],
              [-2.,  2.],
              [ 2., -2.]])

U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(A, full_matrices=False)

A.shape, U.shape, s.shape, Vt.shape
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre><![CDATA[((3, 2), (3, 2), (2,), (2, 2))]]></pre>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What is the shape of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>How many singular values are returned?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why is <c>s.shape</c> equal to <c>(2,)</c> rather than <c>(2, 2)</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What matrix does <c>Vt</c> represent?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>full_matrices=False</c> do in this example?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is <m>3\times 2</m>. The reduced SVD returns two singular values because
            <me>\min(3,2)=2.</me>
            The array <c>s</c> stores only the diagonal entries of <m>\Sigma</m>, not the full diagonal matrix, so its shape is <c>(2,)</c>. The array <c>Vt</c> represents <m>V^T</m>. With <c>full_matrices=False</c>, NumPy returns the reduced SVD shapes needed for reconstruction:
            <me>
              U\in\mathbb R^{3\times 2},
              \qquad
              s\in\mathbb R^2,
              \qquad
              V^T\in\mathbb R^{2\times 2}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-singular-vector-identity">
        <title>Checking a singular-vector identity in code</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For an SVD
            <me>A=U\Sigma V^T,</me>
            the identity
            <me>A\mathbf{v}_i=\sigma_i\mathbf{u}_i</me>
            is one of the main ways to read the factors. The following code checks this identity for a diagonal stretch.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A = np.array([[3., 0.],
              [0., 1.]])

U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(A, full_matrices=False)

i = 0
v = Vt.T[:, i]
lhs = A @ v
rhs = s[i] * U[:, i]

lhs, rhs, np.allclose(lhs, rhs)
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre><![CDATA[(array([3., 0.]), array([3., 0.]), True)]]></pre>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Which vector is stored in <c>v</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>lhs</c> compute?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>rhs</c> compute?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does <c>np.allclose(lhs, rhs)</c> return <c>True</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which stretch factor appears in this computation?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The vector <c>v</c> is the first right singular vector <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. The array <c>lhs</c> computes <m>A\mathbf{v}_1</m>. The array <c>rhs</c> computes <m>\sigma_1\mathbf{u}_1</m>. The value <c>True</c> means that the numerical computation agrees with
            <me>A\mathbf{v}_1=\sigma_1\mathbf{u}_1.</me>
            The stretch factor is
            <me>\sigma_1=3.</me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </subsection>
  </section>

