    <subsection xml:id="u6-exercises-sec-singular-value-decomposition">
      <title>Singular value decomposition</title>
      <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO4, U6-LO6, U6-LO7.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.06%3A_The_Singular_Value_Decomposition/8.6E%3A_The_Singular_Value_Decomposition_Exercises">Nicholson 8.6.9(b)</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-exercises-applied-computational">
      <title>Additional exercises</title>
      <p>
        These exercises connect absolute extrema, Lagrange multipliers with one and several constraints, quadratic forms, maximum stretch, PCA, SVD, rank, and the four fundamental subspaces. Solution sketches are collected in Appendix <xref ref="app-u6-applied-solutions" text="global"/>.
      </p>
      <exercise xml:id="act-u6-recap-choosing-candidates-equations">
        <title>Choosing candidates and equations</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each problem below, state the equations used to find candidates and say which candidates must be compared. Do not solve the equations.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the absolute extrema of a differentiable function <m>f(x,y)</m> on the rectangle <m>D=[a,b]\times[c,d]</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find extrema of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>
                  g(\mathbf{x})=k,
                </me>
                assuming <m>\nabla g\ne\mathbf{0}</m> on the feasible set.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find extrema of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>
                  g_1(\mathbf{x})=k_1,
                  \qquad
                  g_2(\mathbf{x})=k_2,
                </me>
                assuming the two constraint gradients are linearly independent on the feasible set.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the absolute extrema of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> on the closed bounded region
                <me>
                  D=\{\mathbf{x}:g(\mathbf{x})\leq k\},
                </me>
                assuming <m>\nabla g\ne\mathbf{0}</m> on its boundary.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO1, U6-LO2, U6-LO3.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="act-u6-recap-quadratic-form-three-readings">
        <title>One quadratic form, three readings</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              B=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              5 \amp 4\\
              4 \amp 5
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 2\\
              2 \amp 1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              C=\frac12B.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>B</m> and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of
                <me>
                  q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}
                </me>
                subject to
                <me>
                  \|\mathbf{x}\|=1.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
                <me>
                  A^TA=B.
                </me>
                Find the singular values and right singular directions of <m>A</m>. What is
                <me>
                  \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|?
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose <m>C</m> is the covariance matrix from <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>. Find the first and second principal directions and their captured variations.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why do the same two directions appear in all three readings?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO4, U6-LO5, U6-LO6, U5-LO4.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-candidate-table-rectangle">
        <title>Candidate table on a rectangle</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-2x+4y</me>
            on the rectangle
            <me>D=\{(x,y):0\leq x\leq 3,\ -3\leq y\leq 1\}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Find the critical point of <m>f</m> in the interior of <m>D</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the boundary candidates on each of the four edges.</p></li>
            <li><p>Include the four corner points.</p></li>
            <li><p>Make a candidate table with the source of each candidate and the value of <m>f</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum of <m>f</m> on <m>D</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO1.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-parallel-gradients-circle">
        <title>Parallel gradients on a circle</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>f(x,y)=3x+4y</me>
            and constrain the input to the circle
            <me>x^2+y^2=25.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Write the constraint as <m>g(x,y)=25</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Solve
                <me>\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Find the constrained critical points.</p></li>
            <li><p>Evaluate <m>f</m> at the constrained critical points.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which point gives the absolute maximum? Which point gives the absolute minimum?</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain geometrically why <m>\nabla f</m> is parallel to <m>\nabla g</m> at those points.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO2.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-quadratic-form-unit-circle">
        <title>Quadratic form on the unit circle</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              B=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                5\amp 0\\
                0\amp 2
              \end{bmatrix}
            </me>
            and
            <me>q(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.</me>
            Write
            <me>
              \mathbf{x}=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                x\\
                y
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Write <m>q(\mathbf{x})</m> in terms of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Maximize <m>q(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>\|\mathbf{x}\|=1.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Minimize <m>q(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>\|\mathbf{x}\|=1.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Which unit vectors give the extreme values?</p></li>
            <li><p>How do the answers relate to eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>B</m>?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO4.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-maximum-stretch-matrix">
        <title>Maximum stretch of a matrix</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 2\\
                2\amp 1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the singular values of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find a unit right singular vector corresponding to the largest singular value.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is
                <me>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|?</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Which input direction is stretched most?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO4.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-reading-svd">
        <title>Reading an SVD</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose
            <me>A=U\Sigma V^T,</me>
            where
            <me>
              U=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 0\\
                0\amp 1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \Sigma=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                5\amp 0\amp 0\\
                0\amp 2\amp 0
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              V=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1/\sqrt2\amp 0\amp 1/\sqrt2\\
                1/\sqrt2\amp 0\amp -1/\sqrt2\\
                0\amp 1\amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Thus the right singular vectors are
            <me>
              \mathbf{v}_1=
              \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1\\
                0
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                0\\
                0\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_3=
              \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                -1\\
                0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>What are the singular values of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is <m>\operatorname{rank}(A)</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{v}_1</m>, <m>A\mathbf{v}_2</m>, and <m>A\mathbf{v}_3</m> using the SVD.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which input direction is forgotten?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which input directions are transmitted?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO6, U6-LO7.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-fundamental-subspaces-svd">
        <title>Fundamental subspaces from an SVD</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the SVD data from the previous checkpoint.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{row}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{null}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{null}(A^T)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that the dimensions agree with rank-nullity.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO7.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-two-constraints-sphere">
        <title>Two constraints on a sphere</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>f(x,y,z)=x-y.</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Find the absolute extrema of <m>f</m> subject to
            <me>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</me>
            and
            <me>x+y+z=0.</me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Describe the feasible set geometrically. Why do absolute extrema exist?</p></li>
            <li><p>Show that the gradients of the two constraints are linearly independent at every feasible point.</p></li>
            <li><p>Form the Lagrangian using multipliers <m>\lambda</m> and <m>\mu</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write and solve the Lagrange equations.</p></li>
            <li><p>Evaluate <m>f</m> at the candidates and identify the absolute maximum and minimum.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO3.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-principal-directions-centered-data">
        <title>Principal directions of a centered data set</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The rows of
            <me>
              X=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                4\amp2\\
                2\amp4\\
                -2\amp0\\
                0\amp-2
              \end{bmatrix}
            </me>
            are four data points in <m>\R^2</m>.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Compute the mean <m>\bar{\mathbf{x}}</m> and the centered data matrix <m>Z</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the covariance matrix
                <me>C=\frac14Z^TZ.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> and corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors. Choose <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> for the larger eigenvalue and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> for the smaller eigenvalue.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Form
                <me>
                  V=
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                    \mathbf{v}_1\amp\mathbf{v}_2
                  \end{bmatrix}
                </me>
                and compute the principal-coordinate matrix
                <me>T=ZV.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
                <me>V^TCV.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO5.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-pca-reconstruction-regression">
        <title>PCA reconstruction and regression</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the data and principal directions from <xref ref="ex-u6-checkpoint-principal-directions-centered-data" text="custom">the checkpoint “Principal directions of a centered data set”</xref>. The first centered data vector is
            <me>
              \mathbf{z}_1=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                3\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              \mathbf{v}_1
              =
              \frac1{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the first principal-component score
                <me>t_1=\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the projected centered vector
                <me>\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1=t_1\mathbf{v}_1</me>
                and the reconstruction residual
                <me>
                  \mathbf{r}_1
                  =
                  \mathbf{z}_1-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
                <me>\mathbf{r}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_1=0.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the reconstructed original data point
                <me>
                  \widehat{\mathbf{x}}_1
                  =
                  \bar{\mathbf{x}}+\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Give an equation for the PCA line
                <me>\bar{\mathbf{x}}+\spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                For the regression of <m>y</m> on <m>x</m>, use
                <me>
                  m=\frac{C_{12}}{C_{11}},
                  \qquad
                  b=\bar y-m\bar x
                </me>
                to find the regression line.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Why are the PCA and regression lines different? Describe the residual direction minimized by each method.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO5, U4-LO5.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-redundant-features-singular-values">
        <title>Redundant features revisited by singular values</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Return to the Unit 2 redundant-feature matrix
            <me>
              X=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 2\amp 3\\
                2\amp 4\amp 6\\
                0\amp 1\amp 1\\
                1\amp -1\amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            The third column is the sum of the first two columns.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li>
              <p>
                Verify that
                <me>
                  X
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                    1\\
                    1\\
                    -1
                  \end{bmatrix}
                  =
                  \mathbf{0}.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What does this say about <m>\operatorname{null}(X)</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does this say about the rank of <m>X</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What singular value should appear because of this redundancy?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If <m>X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}</m>, what is
                <me>
                  X\left(\mathbf{c}+t
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                    1\\
                    1\\
                    -1
                  \end{bmatrix}\right)?
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Explain how this revisits the Unit 2 idea of nonunique coefficient vectors.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U2-LO6, U6-LO7.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-pca-from-svd">
        <title>PCA from an SVD</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Return to the centered data matrix from <xref ref="ex-u6-checkpoint-principal-directions-centered-data" text="custom">the checkpoint “Principal directions of a centered data set”</xref>. Suppose
            <me>
              Z=U_2\Sigma_2V^T,
            </me>
            where
            <me>
              \mathbf{u}_1
              =
              \frac12
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1\\
                -1\\
                -1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{u}_2
              =
              \frac12
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                -1\\
                -1\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            <me>
              U_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{u}_1\amp\mathbf{u}_2
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \Sigma_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                4\sqrt2\amp0\\
                0\amp2\sqrt2
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              V=
              \frac1{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp1\\
                1\amp-1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use
                <me>\lambda_j=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{4}</me>
                to recover the two covariance eigenvalues.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Which columns of <m>V</m> are the first and second principal directions?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the principal-coordinate matrix
                <me>T=U_2\Sigma_2.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the reconstruction using only the first principal direction:
                <me>
                  \widehat Z_1
                  =
                  \sigma_1\mathbf{u}_1\mathbf{v}_1^T.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
            <li><p>What do the two columns of <m>T</m> represent?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Learning outcomes.</em> U6-LO5, U6-LO7.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u6-highlights">
    <title>Unit 6 highlights</title>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-highlights-reference">
      <title>Mathematical quick reference</title>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Absolute extrema</title>
        <p><alert>Extreme value theorem.</alert> A continuous real-valued function on a nonempty closed bounded subset of <m>\mathbb R^n</m> attains an absolute maximum and an absolute minimum.</p>
        <p><alert>Candidate procedure.</alert> Find interior critical points, examine interior points where differentiability fails, and optimize on every boundary piece. Include endpoints, corners, and intersections of boundary pieces. Compare the function values at all candidates. When a boundary is parametrized by <m>\mathbf r(t)</m>, optimize <m>f(\mathbf r(t))</m> over its parameter domain.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Lagrange multipliers</title>
        <p><alert>One equality constraint.</alert> At a constrained local extremum of <m>f</m> on <m>g(\mathbf x)=k</m>, with <m>f,g</m> continuously differentiable nearby and <m>\nabla g\neq\mathbf0</m>, there is a scalar <m>\lambda</m> such that
          <me>\nabla f(\mathbf x)=\lambda\nabla g(\mathbf x),\qquad g(\mathbf x)=k.</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Several equality constraints.</alert> For <m>g_i(\mathbf x)=k_i</m>, <m>i=1,\ldots,s</m>, with continuously differentiable functions and independent constraint gradients at the extremum,
          <me>\nabla f(\mathbf x)=\sum_{i=1}^s\lambda_i\nabla g_i(\mathbf x),\qquad g_i(\mathbf x)=k_i\quad(i=1,\ldots,s).</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Lagrangian.</alert> Package both the gradient equation and all constraints as
          <me>L(\mathbf x,\boldsymbol\lambda)=f(\mathbf x)-\sum_{i=1}^s\lambda_i\bigl(g_i(\mathbf x)-k_i\bigr),\qquad \nabla_{\mathbf x}L=\mathbf0,\quad L_{\lambda_i}=0.</me>
        </p>
        <p><alert>Use the conditions.</alert> Solve the system for feasible candidates and compare objective values. Examine separately any points where constraint gradients fail the hypotheses, and any additional boundary restrictions. The multiplier equations are necessary under these hypotheses, not sufficient for an extremum.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Quadratic forms and maximum stretch</title>
        <p><alert>Unit-sphere extrema.</alert> For a real symmetric <m>B</m>,
          <me>\min_{\|\mathbf x\|=1}\mathbf x^TB\mathbf x=\lambda_{\min}(B),\qquad \max_{\|\mathbf x\|=1}\mathbf x^TB\mathbf x=\lambda_{\max}(B).</me>
          Unit eigenvectors for the extreme eigenvalues attain the extrema.</p>
        <p><alert>Matrix stretch.</alert> For <m>A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}</m>,
          <me>\|A\mathbf x\|^2=\mathbf x^TA^TA\mathbf x,\qquad \max_{\|\mathbf x\|=1}\|A\mathbf x\|=\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(A^TA)}=\sigma_1.</me>
          The positive singular values are square roots of the positive eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Principal directions and orthogonal reconstruction</title>
        <p><alert>Centering and covariance.</alert> For <m>X\in\mathbb R^{n\times d}</m> with data rows <m>\mathbf x_i^T</m>, let <m>\boldsymbol\mu=\frac1n\sum_i\mathbf x_i</m> and let <m>Z</m> have rows <m>(\mathbf x_i-\boldsymbol\mu)^T</m>. This book uses
          <me>C=\frac1nZ^TZ.</me>
          The symmetric matrix <m>C</m> has nonnegative eigenvalues.</p>
        <p><alert>Principal directions.</alert> Choose orthonormal eigenvectors <m>\mathbf v_1,\ldots,\mathbf v_d</m> of <m>C</m>, ordered by <m>\lambda_1\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_d\geq0</m>. The first direction maximizes <m>\mathbf v^TC\mathbf v</m> over unit vectors; each later direction maximizes it subject to orthogonality to earlier directions.</p>
        <p><alert>Scores and reconstruction.</alert> Put <m>V_k=[\mathbf v_1\ \cdots\ \mathbf v_k]</m>. Then
          <me>T_k=ZV_k,\qquad \widehat Z=T_kV_k^T=ZV_kV_k^T,\qquad R=Z-\widehat Z,\quad RV_k=0.</me>
          Rows of <m>\widehat Z</m> are projections onto the chosen subspace. Add the mean vector to recover approximations in the original coordinates.</p>
        <p><alert>Captured variation.</alert> When total variation is positive, the fraction retained by the first <m>k</m> directions is
          <me>\frac{\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_k}{\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d}.</me>
          Maximizing the sum of squared scores is equivalent to minimizing the sum of squared reconstruction residuals. A repeated eigenvalue permits different orthonormal choices within its eigenspace.</p>
      </paragraphs>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Singular value decomposition</title>
        <p><alert>Full SVD.</alert> Every real <m>m\times n</m> matrix has a factorization
          <me>A=U\Sigma V^T,\qquad U\in\mathbb R^{m\times m},\quad\Sigma\in\mathbb R^{m\times n},\quad V\in\mathbb R^{n\times n},</me>
          with <m>U,V</m> orthogonal and rectangular diagonal <m>\Sigma</m>. For <m>p=\min(m,n)</m>, its diagonal entries satisfy <m>\sigma_1\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_p\geq0</m>.</p>
        <p><alert>Construction and action.</alert> Choose an orthonormal eigenvector basis of <m>A^TA</m> for the columns of <m>V</m>. For each positive singular value, set <m>\mathbf u_i=A\mathbf v_i/\sigma_i</m>; complete these to an orthonormal basis of <m>\mathbb R^m</m>. If <m>r=\operatorname{rank}(A)</m>, then
          <me>A\mathbf v_i=\sigma_i\mathbf u_i\ (1\leq i\leq r),\qquad A\mathbf v_i=\mathbf0\ (r\lt i\leq n),</me>
          <me>A=\sum_{i=1}^r\sigma_i\mathbf u_i\mathbf v_i^T.</me>
          Do not divide by a zero singular value.</p>
        <p><alert>Fundamental subspaces.</alert> In a full SVD,
          <me>\operatorname{col}(A)=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf u_1,\ldots,\mathbf u_r\},\qquad \operatorname{null}(A^T)=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf u_{r+1},\ldots,\mathbf u_m\},</me>
          <me>\operatorname{row}(A)=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_1,\ldots,\mathbf v_r\},\qquad \operatorname{null}(A)=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_{r+1},\ldots,\mathbf v_n\}.</me>
          Empty lists give the zero subspace. The number of positive singular values is the rank.</p>
        <p><alert>PCA from an SVD.</alert> For centered <m>Z=U\Sigma V^T</m>,
          <me>C=V\left(\frac1n\Sigma^T\Sigma\right)V^T,\qquad T=ZV=U\Sigma.</me>
          Thus principal directions are right singular vectors, and corresponding covariance eigenvalues are <m>\sigma_i^2/n</m>, with additional zeros when needed.</p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-highlights-mistakes">
      <title>Common mistakes</title>
      <p>Ignoring boundaries or constraint equations; treating every multiplier solution as an extremum; failing to compare objective values; applying the multiplier theorem when constraint gradients are dependent; or requiring the objective gradient to be parallel to every constraint gradient separately.</p>
      <p>Dropping the unit-length condition in a quadratic-form extremum; forgetting to center before forming covariance; selecting the smallest eigenvalue for the first principal direction; confusing a direction, a score, and a reconstructed vector; dividing by a zero singular value; interchanging left and right singular vectors; or using an incomplete set of singular vectors to describe a null space.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-highlights-connections">
      <title>Connections</title>
      <p><xref ref="u1-vectors-matrices-linear-maps" text="custom">Unit 1</xref> supplies vectors and matrix actions. <xref ref="u2-anatomy-linear-map" text="custom">Unit 2</xref> supplies rank and fundamental spaces. <xref ref="u3-beyond-linear-maps" text="custom">Unit 3</xref> supplies gradients, level sets, and closed bounded regions. <xref ref="u4-orthogonality-least-squares" text="custom">Unit 4</xref> supplies orthogonal projection. <xref ref="u5-optimization-training" text="custom">Unit 5</xref> supplies symmetric diagonalization and quadratic forms. <xref ref="u7-abstract-vector-spaces-polynomial-approximation" text="custom">Unit 7</xref> extends orthogonality and projection to polynomial spaces.</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
